 ## Chat, tools & boot surfaces
 
-- The transcript and composer are built on `@assistant-ui/react`. Extend the
-  existing components under `src/components/assistant-ui` and
-  `src/app/chat/composer`; do not fork a second markdown, message, tool-call, or
-  approval renderer for one feature.
-- **Inline widgets** — a tool result that renders as a panel the user reads or
-  acts on (clarify, artifact card) wears `WIDGET_SHELL_CLASS`
-  (`src/components/chat/widget-shell.ts`): shared radius, the
-  `--ui-widget-surface-background` fill, no border. Its actions sit *outside*
-  the panel, below it. Don't give one widget its own radius or fill.
-- Bordered surfaces in the transcript (tables, fences, callouts, attachments)
-  use `--ui-stroke-tertiary`. Not `border-border` — that's the app-wide
-  default and reads too hot against the thread.
-- Interactive directive chips in the composer expose their action on hover.
-  The action stays visible for a 500ms grace period while the pointer crosses
-  from the chip to the floating pill; leaving both dismisses it.
-- A tool result may expose an inline action that opens a preview. It must not
-  open the rail automatically.
-- Tool rows reserve destructive red for explicit failures. Missing read paths and
-  ambiguous exit-1 results use neutral notices, with details still available.
-  Errors described inside returned data are not tool failures. Expanded failures
-  show the actual explanation; supporting output keeps its normal text color.
-- Nested transcript scrollers keep their height caps and hand vertical scrolling
-  back to the thread at either edge (`overscroll-behavior-y: auto`), even when
-  their content fits. Only the outer thread contains vertical overscroll;
-  horizontal code/output boundaries may remain contained. Thinking previews
-  follow new tokens only while near the bottom, preserving the user's reading
-  position until they scroll back down.
-- Composer status groups start collapsed except todos. Progress updates and queue
-  pause/resume preserve the user's disclosure choice. Error banners meet the
-  stack's top edge without a blank padding strip. File and preview links remain
-  visible at the bottom of the stack, below the queue and all status groups.
-  A centered ridge on the composer's top edge hides/reveals the entire stack,
-  including the git row, with a short downward/upward drawer slide. Its choice
-  persists per conversation and owner, not globally. Hidden sections stay
-  mounted but inert so their disclosure choices survive; reduced motion is instant.
-- Popping out a composer makes it the window's only visible composer. It keeps
-  its viewport placement while hover or keyboard focus selects a chat pane;
-  moving back into the editor retains that recipient. Drafts, attachments and
-  queues stay session-owned. Docking restores the individual pane composers.
-  In either placement, moving into a chat pane gives its editor typing focus
-  immediately and preserves its caret. Layout-only hover events and delayed
-  focus callbacks cannot replace that choice, and a live transcript selection
-  is never cleared by focus-follow. Movement within the same pane
-  must not flush React; deliberate Tab navigation and clicked controls still work.
-  An inline message edit is a typing target of its own: opening one keeps focus
-  in the edit editor, and neither its mount-time focus nor mouse movement while
-  it is open hands the caret back to the pane composer.
-  Active dictation or voice conversation pins the recipient until capture ends,
-  keeping the microphone's stop controls and shortcut attached to its owner.
-- Status-stack rows use `StatusRow` with a leading `dismiss` action, a state
-  icon and optional trailing actions. `StatusDismissButton` owns the Codicon
-  close button for previews, background tasks and queued prompts; do not swap
-  it for a trash icon or a CSS glyph. Icons and controls align to the first text
-  line, including messages with attachment metadata.
-- `status-stack.css` owns the shared columns and `0.25rem` nesting step. Rows
-  own their padding and full-width hover fill. `StatusControlRow` uses the same
-  columns for goal/loop/heartbeat details; `StatusPendingIcon` supplies the
-  dashed marker for tasks and criteria. The first row keeps its normal padding;
-  the stack adds no extra top inset.
-- Keep the rounded status card stationary, with the bounded scroll viewport
-  inside it. The outer scroll boundary uses `overscroll-behavior-y: contain`;
-  nested rosters and transcripts use `auto` so wheel input can hand off at an
-  edge without trapping it or scrolling the chat behind the stack.
-- Install, onboarding, connecting, boot failure, and reauthentication are
-  distinct states with shared visual primitives. Preserve their recovery
-  semantics when unifying appearance.
-- Guide startup has a dedicated landing screen with the shared long-operation
-  Loader and a localized startup label. It remains active until the guide's
-  saved conversation is loaded. No greeting, composer, Skip setup, or statusbar
-  appears early. Reduced motion uses a static BrandMark and the same label.
-  On readiness, reveal the real conversation and fade controls in over 100ms.
-  Never fabricate progress, delay readiness for motion, or replace a resumed
-  conversation with a new greeting.
-- Respect `AppShell` overlay ownership. Persistent terminal/content layers,
-  route overlays, dialogs, and boot surfaces must not compete through ad-hoc
-  z-index literals. Pick a rung of the ladder in `styles.css` instead —
-  `--z-modal-backdrop` / `--z-modal` / `--z-modal-popover`, `--z-over-modal`
-  (toasts, tooltips, command surfaces) and `--z-over-modal-content`,
-  `--z-switcher-backdrop` / `--z-switcher`, then the boot chain
-  `--z-connecting` → `--z-onboarding` → `--z-setup` → `--z-crash`. Plain
-  `z-10`/`z-20` are still right for stacking *within* one component.
+- **The chrome frames the chat, the sidebar stands apart.** In Pebbl dark the
+  top band above the reading column, the collapsed rail on the right and the
+  statusbar's stretch under the chat paint with the editor surface (`#191919`),
+  the same as the transcript; the sidebar column, the band above it and the
+  statusbar segment under it keep the sidebar surface (`#252627`). The
+  statusbar is one full-width element, so it splits with a gradient at
+  `--sidebar-column-width` — published by `useSidebarColumnWidth` from a
+  ResizeObserver on `[data-slot='sidebar']` — and the seam follows the panel
+  when it is dragged. Zone headers decide by `sidebarGroup` (a zone holding
+  only side/tool panes keeps the sidebar surface); a collapsed rail decides by
+  `railSide`, and carries `--pane-tab-strip-bg` so the vertical tab inside it
+  matches. Don't bake a pixel here: the column is resizable. Chrome controls
+  hover neutrally (`--ui-control-hover-background: #2b2c2d`); the approval-mode
+  chip rests on that token, so an accent-tinted hover reads as a stray light
+  pill on the dark bar. Tooltips are inverted at the token level
+  (`bg-foreground`); the dark block repaints `.tooltip-bubble` and its arrow to
+  `#262728` with a `#3b3c3d` hairline instead of leaving a white card.
+- **The sidebar has no tab strip; Sessions / Bots are its own top-right
+  switches.** The Sessions/Bots panes are hide-only chrome, and `sideChromeOnly`
+  makes their zone stripless whatever mode a saved layout asks for, so the strip
+  tests became "no strip" tests. The switch itself renders **inside the sidebar
+  zone's own header band** (`tree-group`, `topEdge && sidebarGroup`) — that band
+  is chrome the shell already carves out of the macOS drag map — and it must be a
+  LAYOUT sibling of `data-window-drag-handle`, never an absolute box on top of
+  it. An overlapping drag box swallows hover and click on macOS whatever
+  `no-drag` the button asks for, and it comes back on the next launch once a
+  saved layout restores the header. The handle's `flex-1` shrinks around the
+  pair instead. Two 24px buttons with codicon glyphs (`comment-discussion`,
+  `robot`) call `revealTreePane` for `sessions` / `hermes-bots:pane`, both
+  resting at `text-foreground`. Glyph size is set by INK, not by box: the
+  codicons draw ~72% of their em box where `layout-sidebar-left` fills 86%, so
+  the switch uses 16px against the toggle's 13.9px to land the same 12px ink. Messaging was dropped
+  from the nav list, its rows use the project/session row typography (13px,
+  weight 400), and New session leads with a `+`.
+- **The boot splash hands off to the connecting overlay, mark to mark.**
+  `GatewayConnectingOverlay` used to spell CONNECTING in `--theme-primary` with a
+  decode cursor — the loudest thing on a screen the user cannot act on. It now
+  renders the same `BrandMark sweep` at the same 64px the splash uses, so the
+  logo is continuous from first paint until the gateway opens. No copy on that
+  screen; the slow-setup card and the boot-failure surface keep their words,
+  because those ask for something.
+- **The boot splash lives in `index.html`, not in React.** `#boot-splash` is
+  markup + inline CSS in the document (an 88px mark, same draw-on sweep on a
+  1.5s cycle) because the window paints before the bundle arrives — anything
+  that waits for mount is already too late, and a React splash would only ever
+  appear once there was nothing left to wait for. `dismissBootSplash()` in
+  `src/main.tsx` fades it out two frames after the shell mounts. Transparent
+  windows (`hud`/`overlay`/`quick`/`wake`) remove it immediately. No logo means
+  no branded boot screen existed before this: the BrandMark sweep only ever
+  reached the update/About surfaces.
+- **Loading surfaces carry the draw-on sweep.** `BrandMark sweep` runs a tight
+  white-core/accent-shoulder band across the mark once per 4s, clipped to the
+  icon's rounded box. Never mask it with the PNG: a `mask-image` pointing at a
+  `file://` asset resolves to an EMPTY mask in this renderer (the artwork paints
+  as `<img>`, the same URL as a mask comes back transparent — verified, the band
+  never drew), and inlining a data: URL costs a build step for a 12px highlight.
+  Loading surfaces only (boot/update/install) — a badge sitting in a list must
+  not be in motion.
+- **A nested row is name-only at REST, not while it is working.** The project
+  tree's nested rows render an empty 14px lead so titles align with the project
+  label; that also meant a busy session there had no status dot at all, so the
+  pulse was invisible wherever the tree lists sessions. The lead renders
+  `SessionStatusDot` when the state is active (`working`, `stalled`,
+  `needs-input`, `background`) — same 14px box, so nothing shifts — and stays
+  empty otherwise. `unread` deliberately stays out: a finished session keeps the
+  tree clean.
+- **Busy sessions pulse the status dot, not the row.** The row used to wrap a
+  travelling `.arc-border.arc-row` around its whole box. Busy is now
+  `.session-dot-pulse` on `SessionStatusDot` (`working` only): a halo expands out
+  of the 6px dot to 2.1× and fades while the dot itself brightens toward white
+  and pops 12%, every 1.4s. The brighten is the legible part — an opacity dip at
+  6px reads as a rendering hiccup. `stalled` stays hollow and still on purpose. The row background, border and title
+  stay still, and the motion is the same primitive the pane tabs and the session
+  switcher already render.
+- **No empty-slot reaction affordance.** A message never draws a reaction
+  control on its own: the ☺ appeared the moment the pointer crossed a turn, which
+  read as clutter under the sent bubble. `data-slot="aui_msg-reactions"` renders
+  only for a LANDED reaction (full strength, clickable to switch or retract).
+  Reacting lives on gestures — double-click tapback, right-click on a sent bubble.
+- **The sidebar's "open elsewhere" band means on screen, not merely owned.**
+  Session rows paint `--ui-row-active-background` when selected and the weaker
+  `--ui-row-open-background` for the other tiles in the workspace, so a
+  multi-pane layout shows every chat on screen. Parked tiles are excluded from
+  `$openStoredSessionIds`: they stay owned by a zone but unmounted, so a row the
+  user had closed out of sight no longer wears a highlight they never made.
+- **The minimal bottom bar exempts the approval chip.** `minimalBottomBar`
+  filters the statusbar down to the controls that carry state you cannot infer:
+  the approval-mode chip (whether the agent may act on its own) and the ⌘, which
+  now lives in the titlebar. Filtering on `command-center` alone emptied the bar
+  once that item moved out of it — never filter the approval chip, it is a safety
+  readout, not decoration. Guarded by `statusbar-visibility.test.tsx`.
+- **Zone chrome follows the active pane's surface.** `PaneChrome.surface`
+  (`'editor' | 'sidebar'`, absent = the zone default) tells the sidebar zone
+  what the active pane paints: Bots declares `editor`, so its titlebar band and
+  the statusbar strip under it paint the chat colour instead of staying
+  sidebar-grey. The statusbar reads it through `--sidebar-chrome-surface`, which
+  the sidebar's own `TreeGroup` publishes on `:root` (only that group writes it —
+  another group's cleanup would wipe it). Bots roster rows carry the same 10px
+  corner as bubbles and sidebar rows.
+- **One corner radius across floating surfaces.** 10px — chat bubbles, sidebar
+  row hover (`SidebarRowShell`), the Settings/Command Center card
+  (`OverlayView`), and every floating list (`menuSurfaceClass`, context menus).
+  The overlay's own nav rows (`overlay-split-layout`, `panel`) match the sidebar
+  rows; menu rows inside a list carry 6px so the highlight nests.
 
